{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2613 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "36653f92f6099f64e8ba58d466cceff0e7a98a80aec068d38e5eef515b7cc8e9" :edb/db-before-t 2612 :edb/db-after-t 2613 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535925 50 #inst "2026-10-09T10:48:37.087Z" 13194139535925 true] [17592186151861 1100 "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$." 13194139535925 false] [17592186151861 1100 "Let $R = [0,2] \\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186151861 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/64b9a595613bbab374eb084adcd919f8.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/cf15a413bc2fd256a2782ed519c67d13.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/8877f481ed010f430a9590b25e256f00.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{11} &= f(0, 1) = 2 \\\\\nm_{21} &= f(1, 1) = 3 \\\\\nm_{12} &= f(0, 2) = 4 \\\\\nm_{22} &= f(1, 2) = 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L$, we get\n\n$$\nL(f, P) = 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 14\n$$" 13194139535925 false] [17592186151861 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0ad490af48e1db93ab9cae7e482b5eb8956aa14f8d2972816f8852179b35c4cb.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a33927647d7651ce47c9e6619bd26a29ceb5cc5dd74e379bf81ea61806fb5c95.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each bottom left corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)=2+3+4+5=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186151862 1100 "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$." 13194139535925 false] [17592186151862 1100 "Let $R = [1,3] \\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186151862 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/46cc42002ea537e8af73cd4dc56915ef.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1\n$$\n\nfor all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/5c7c567ad1a1236e0bed12cbb74d8657.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/2c0d05fce61626b7c70063064b2eb549.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{11} &= f(2, 1) = 15 \\\\\nm_{21} &= f(3, 1) = 10 \\\\\nm_{12} &= f(2, 2) = 12 \\\\\nm_{22} &= f(3, 2) = 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L$, we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44\n$$" 13194139535925 false] [17592186151862 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot\\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\textrm{Area}(R_{ij}) = 1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12} + m_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y) = 20-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/9fa44875a37e8c7b7bc69b175ac1f30fe73af936565e68ecaa82e02b7617b330.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/595d13ca8ec18056038a855e96ca4a3d8cc967182dae5c54b9746c731238413b.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,1)=15 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,1)=10 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,2)=12 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(3,2)=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL(f,P) = 15+10+12+7 = 44.\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186151968 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/8de3a1512666201ab8d9ab7c06385dac08acb67345273a2e07252616f666cf27.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition." 13194139535925 true] [17592186151968 1100 "Consider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R$. Express the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2613/Source/Images/bdb990e68c9b789f02af33ceaed8ea6b.png)" 13194139535925 false] [17592186151968 1145 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}$.\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}$. We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22})\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 3 \\cdot m_{11} + 3 \\cdot m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22} \\\\\n&= 3m_{11} + 3m_{21} + m_{12} + m_{22}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 false] [17592186151968 1145 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $4$ rectangular solids, one for each $R_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion $R_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\nm_{ij}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{ij})\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion $R_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL = m_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) + m_{21}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) + m_{12}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) + m_{22}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{11}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{21}) = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(R_{12}) = \\textrm{Area}(R_{22}) = 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22} \\\\  & =3{m}_{11}+3{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186152071 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374744 13194139535925 true] [17592186152071 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374751 13194139535925 true] [17592186152071 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374758 13194139535925 true] [17592186152071 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374765 13194139535925 true] [17592186152072 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374723 13194139535925 true] [17592186152072 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374730 13194139535925 true] [17592186152072 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374737 13194139535925 true] [17592186152072 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374772 13194139535925 true] [17592186152156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374688 13194139535925 true] [17592186152156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374695 13194139535925 true] [17592186152156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374702 13194139535925 true] [17592186152156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374709 13194139535925 true] [17592186152156 1020 #edb/ref 17592186374716 13194139535925 true] [17592186152631 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535925 true] [17592186152631 1100 "Let $R = [1, 3] \\cdot [1, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = x + y + 1$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {1, 2, 3}, P_{2} = {1, 2, 3}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139535925 false] [17592186152631 1100 "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={1,2,3},\\,{P}_{2}={1,2,3},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186152631 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/46b590842cb6dd1cb994318be3a35ef0f7782542d3a74c4f788180577ab50301.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/193d8dc290095a750fe6c7fb6a5d6e75efb342e213f8aabc8fb7921cd335242e.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/a8c9191c43ca304fee01dbb4298c5312f1993ebd25aef266dd9a4828af5f25d5.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=4 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=4 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =3+4+4+5 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186152632 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535925 true] [17592186152632 1100 "Let $R = [0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5 - x - y$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {0, 1, 2}, P_{2} = {0, 1, 2}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." 13194139535925 false] [17592186152632 1100 "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=5-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}={0,1,2},\\,{P}_{2}={0,1,2},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186152632 1145 "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/3cac974f7352243bfbe008ad8af58ae8222709f81f434070383cc759a46747c7.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=5-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0fefe892c74e8d391e56ee822f83e7e0fe0110593c9d6a4b93b8cfe1e558ab16.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/fe872f1f1b0307760dcdba6ac6f6be20f197662ac250ab46dc77fa472295906f.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=3+2+2+1=8.\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1017 #edb/ref 17592186374689 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1089 "q-122876" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072149/assets/0abae6be42361d157d7cbd46f602d3a0d2044cfec551c61a2739ff9b56798cc3.png)\n\nConsider the partition of the rectangular region $R$ shown above and the positive function $f(x,y)$ defined on $R.$ Which of the following gives an expression for the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to this partition.\n\n*Hint: ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}$*" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1113 #uuid "0fc86049-64cc-5cd7-9cd9-a6d9803210f4" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374688 1145 "To calculate the lower Riemann sum $L$ with respect to the given partition, we compute the sum of the volumes of $3$ rectangular solids, one for each ${R}_{ij}.$\n\nThe volume of the rectangular solid corresponding to each subregion ${R}_{ij}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{m}_{ij}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{ij}).\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ denotes the minimum value of $f$ on the subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nSo in our case, we have\n\n$$\nL={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13}).\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}({R}_{11})=\\text{Area}({R}_{12})=\\text{Area}({R}_{13})=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\cdot {m}_{11}+3\\cdot {m}_{12}+3\\cdot {m}_{13} \\\\  & =3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374690 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374691 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374692 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374693 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1000 #edb/ref 17592186374694 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1041 "selection" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1104 #edb/ref 17592186374692 13194139535925 true] [17592186374689 1110 #uuid "e396a8d4-801c-5f9b-9738-7747e0d05163" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374690 1034 "{m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13}" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374690 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535925 true] [17592186374690 1111 #uuid "29cc7d83-a830-5a1c-8e3c-218d8330e430" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374691 1034 "2({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374691 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535925 true] [17592186374691 1111 #uuid "dcd07bf3-bb8c-534f-a5fe-e8290031d9ca" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374692 1034 "3({m}_{11}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{13})" 13194139535925 true] [17592186374692 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 1319413